# FIREBASE CLIENT CONFIGURATION (PUBLIC PROJECT IDENTIFIERS)
# Set these in ignored .env.local and your deployment/build environment.
# Firebase web configuration remains public in the compiled client bundle.
EXPO_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_APP_ID=
EXPO_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_API_KEY=
EXPO_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID=
EXPO_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_AUTH_DOMAIN=
EXPO_PUBLIC_FIREBASE_MESSAGING_SENDER_ID=
# No Firebase Storage, uploads, payments, or Cloud Functions are configured.

# SET UP YOUR PRIVATE .env
# 1. From the project root, copy this file: cp .env.example .env
#    If .env already exists, edit it instead of overwriting it.
# 2. Follow the instructions for each registrar you want to use below.
# 3. In .env, remove the leading "# " from the variable lines you need and
#    paste each value after "=". Example format: NAMECHEAP_USERNAME=your_username
#    Leave unused registrar variables commented out. Keep instructions commented.
# 4. Keep actual credentials in .env only; this example must stay free of secrets.
#    .env is ignored by Git. Never prefix registrar secrets with EXPO_PUBLIC_.
# 5. Restart the Expo server after changing .env. Deployed servers need these
#    private variables configured in their hosting environment.
#
# CURRENT APP: complete server-side credential groups enable public Domain
# Search, including for signed-out visitors. Search and extension catalogs use
# these values without exposing them to the client. Partial/invalid groups are
# ignored. GoDaddy accepts a PAT or a complete key/secret pair; Namecheap needs
# all three values. Optional inventory fields do not affect public search.
# For account inventory imports, enter each value in its matching labeled input
# in Profile > Connections. Enter values only, without variable names or "=".
# Any signed-in user can choose Import From .env on Profile > Connections.
# Drop or choose a local file to fill the matching inputs automatically, or paste
# its contents and choose Load Keys. Import Server .env loads supported server
# keys into those inputs too. Imports only fill unsaved fields; they do not save
# connections or sync domains. Only supported registrar keys are loaded.
# Review or complete the fields, then choose Save All Connections, or Save & Sync
# for one connection. Existing connections and edits are kept; duplicates are skipped.
# Saving inventory connections, signing in, or restoring a session checks registrar APIs
# through the Expo server and imports domains into your account's table.
# Inventory sync uses saved per-account values, including explicitly imported keys.
# Populating .env alone does not automatically connect a registrar to your account.

# VERCEL: API ACCESS TOKEN AND OPTIONAL TEAM ID
# Docs: https://vercel.com/kb/guide/how-do-i-use-a-vercel-api-access-token
# Registrar API: https://vercel.com/docs/domains/registrar-api
# 1. Sign in to the Vercel account with access to your domains, then open:
#    https://vercel.com/account/settings/tokens
# 2. Name the token (e.g. Domains Database) and choose its scope. For team
#    domains, select the owning team and All Projects; for personal domains,
#    choose Full Account. A single-project scope cannot read team inventory.
# 3. Choose an expiration, select Create, and copy the token immediately;
#    its value is displayed only once.
# 4. In .env, uncomment VERCEL_API_TOKEN and paste the token after "=".
#    The app sends it as Authorization: Bearer <token> for authenticated search.
# 5. To explicitly target a team, copy Team ID from that team's Settings >
#    General and paste it into VERCEL_TEAM_ID. Use the ID beginning with team_,
#    not the team name, slug, or project ID. Leave it commented for personal
#    domains; team-scoped tokens already identify their team.
# 6. For inventory sync, enter the token and optional team ID in their matching
#    fields in Profile > Connections > Vercel.
#    The app imports Vercel-registered domains and excludes external domains
#    that are only attached to Vercel hosting.
# VERCEL_API_TOKEN=
# VERCEL_TEAM_ID=

# GODADDY: PERSONAL ACCESS TOKEN (recommended for new Domains integrations)
# Docs: https://developer.godaddy.com/en/docs/api-users/auth/how-to
# Scopes: https://developer.godaddy.com/en/docs/api-users/auth
# 1. Sign in with the GoDaddy account that owns your domains:
#    https://developer.godaddy.com/personal-access-token
# 2. Select Generate Token, name it (e.g. Domains Database), and set an expiry.
# 3. Expand Domains & DNS and choose domains.domain:read for reading your inventory.
# 4. Generate and copy the token immediately; it is displayed only once.
# 5. In .env, uncomment GODADDY_PAT and paste the token after "=".
#    The search relay sends it as Authorization: Bearer <token>.
# GODADDY_PAT=

# GODADDY: CLASSIC KEY / SECRET (for APIs that require the legacy credential)
# Docs: https://classic-developer.godaddy.com/getstarted
# 1. Sign in at https://classic-developer.godaddy.com/keys and create a key.
# 2. For your real domains, choose Production, not the OTE/test environment.
# 3. Copy the key and its matching secret into the two .env variables below.
#    These are API credentials, not your GoDaddy account password.
# 4. Use this pair only if the backend uses an API accepting classic sso-key auth.
#    Domains v3 requires a PAT; classic keys do not work with v3.
# GODADDY_API_KEY=
# GODADDY_API_SECRET=

# GODADDY: OPTIONAL CUSTOMER UUID OR SHOPPER ID FOR RENEWAL ESTIMATES
# Docs: https://developer.godaddy.com/en/docs/api-users/domains/manage/renewals
# Customer lookup: https://developer.godaddy.com/en/docs/api-users/domains/manage/forwarding
# In Profile > Connections > GoDaddy, use either the Customer UUID field for
# the UUID returned by the Shoppers API or the Shopper ID field for your numeric
# shopper ID (up to 10 digits, found in your GoDaddy account settings), never both.
# The app resolves a shopper ID with GET /v1/shoppers/{id}?includes=customerId;
# when both optional IDs are blank, it tries /v1/shoppers/MY instead.
# A customer UUID skips this lookup. If your PAT cannot read shoppers, also
# provide the classic key/secret pair, or use the resolved customer UUID.
# Optional inventory IDs are excluded from server .env search credentials.
# Renewal estimates are before taxes/fees; the API does not provide the term.
# GODADDY_CUSTOMER_ID=
# GODADDY_SHOPPER_ID=

# HOSTINGER: API TOKEN
# Docs: https://www.hostinger.com/support/10840865-what-is-hostinger-api/
# 1. Sign in at https://hpanel.hostinger.com/ with the account owning your domains.
# 2. In the dashboard sidebar, open Dev Tools > API.
# 3. Choose Generate Token, enter a name, select an expiry, and generate it.
# 4. Copy the token before refreshing; it will not be displayed again.
# 5. In .env, uncomment HOSTINGER_API_TOKEN and paste the token after "=".
# HOSTINGER_API_TOKEN=
# The hosting API automatically includes eligible external hosted domains.
# No external-domain list is required; existing saved lists remain compatible.
# Registrar renewal settings do not come from the hosting API.
# HOSTINGER_EXTERNAL_DOMAINS=example.com,example.net

# PORKBUN: FREE ACCOUNT API
# Profile Connections requires this app's Pro plan or Owner role.
# Docs: https://porkbun.com/api/json/v3/documentation
# 1. Generate the API key and secret at https://porkbun.com/account/api.
# 2. In Profile > Connections > Porkbun, enter each value in its matching field.
# 3. Enable per-domain API access when needed, or opt in all owned domains.
# 4. If this key has source-IP restrictions, permit the relay server's IP.
# PORKBUN_API_KEY=
# PORKBUN_SECRET_API_KEY=

# NAMESILO: FREE MAIN-ACCOUNT API
# Profile Connections requires this app's Pro plan or Owner role.
# Docs: https://www.namesilo.com/api-reference
# 1. Generate a key in your main account's API Manager; subaccounts cannot use it.
# 2. Enter it in the API Key field in Profile > Connections > NameSilo.
#    Optional IP restrictions must permit the relay server. Automated inventory
#    sync uses NameSilo's /apibatch API.
# NAMESILO_API_KEY=

# NAMECHEAP: API KEY, ACCOUNT USERNAME, AND PUBLIC SERVER IP
# Setup: https://www.namecheap.com/support/api/intro/
# Requirements: https://www.namecheap.com/support/knowledgebase/article.aspx/9739/63/api-faq/
# Parameters: https://www.namecheap.com/support/api/global-parameters/
# 1. Sign in to your production Namecheap account (not sandbox).
# 2. Open Profile > Tools > Business & Dev Tools > Namecheap API Access > Manage.
# 3. Enable API access, accept the terms, confirm your password, and copy the key.
# 4. Paste that key into NAMECHEAP_API_KEY in .env, and your account login username
#    into NAMECHEAP_USERNAME. For your own account, the backend uses this username
#    for both Namecheap's ApiUser and UserName parameters.
# 5. On the same page, edit Whitelisted IPs, add the public IPv4 address of the
#    backend making requests, and save. Put that same IP in NAMECHEAP_CLIENT_IP.
#    Use the server's outbound public IP, not localhost or a private LAN address.
#    For a backend on your Mac, use your internet connection's public IPv4 address;
#    update the whitelist and .env if it changes or you deploy to another server.
# 6. Production access requires any one: 20+ domains, a $50+ account balance, or
#    $50+ spent in the last two years. Contact Namecheap support if ineligible.
#    Sandbox credentials are separate and cannot read your real production domains.
# NAMECHEAP_API_KEY=
# NAMECHEAP_USERNAME=
# NAMECHEAP_CLIENT_IP=

# SQUARESPACE: COMMERCE API KEY (REFERENCE ONLY; NOT USED BY THIS APP)
# Setup: https://support.squarespace.com/hc/en-us/articles/236297987-Squarespace-API-keys
# Docs: https://developers.squarespace.com/commerce-apis/authentication-and-permissions
# 1. Sign in to the Squarespace website you want to access. Commerce API keys
#    are website-specific and require an eligible site plan; see the setup guide.
# 2. Open the Developer tools panel, then Developer API Keys > Generate Key.
# 3. Enter a key name, choose the required Commerce API permissions, and select
#    Next. Copy the key immediately; it is displayed only once.
# 4. SQUARESPACE_API_KEY below is an optional placeholder for a future Commerce
#    integration. Leave it commented: this app does not read Commerce API keys.
#    Commerce keys cannot sync your domain portfolio.
# SQUARESPACE_API_KEY=

# SQUARESPACE: DEVELOPER APP OAUTH CREDENTIALS (SAVED ONLY)
# Dashboard: https://account.squarespace.com/developer-apps
# Docs: https://developers.squarespace.com/commerce-apis/oauth
# 1. Register your OAuth app through the Developer Apps dashboard and obtain
#    its client ID and client secret after Squarespace's registration review.
# 2. In Profile > Connections > Squarespace, enter each value in its matching
#    Client ID or Client Secret field. This app only saves these credentials;
#    it does not implement OAuth consent, authorization-code exchange, or
#    domain sync with them. These are separate from approved reseller credentials.
# SQUARESPACE_CLIENT_ID=
# SQUARESPACE_CLIENT_SECRET=

# SQUARESPACE: DOMAINS RESELLER API (PRIVATE ACCOUNT CONNECTION)
# Setup: https://developers.squarespace.com/reseller/api-fundamentals
# Quick start: https://developers.squarespace.com/reseller/quick-start-test-calls
# Inventory: https://developers.squarespace.com/reseller/domains
# Account linking: https://developers.squarespace.com/reseller/account-linking
# 1. Arrange an approved reseller agreement with Squarespace. Your Partner
#    Solutions Architect issues an OAuth client ID and matching client secret.
# 2. In Profile > Connections > Squarespace, enter each approved reseller value
#    in its matching Reseller Client ID or Reseller Client Secret field, then
#    choose Connect & Sync. Do not use Developer Apps credentials or a Commerce key.
# 3. The app exchanges the credentials for a short-lived Bearer token on the
#    server and imports that reseller's provisioned domain inventory.
#    Registration/expiry dates are imported when supplied. Auto-renew and cost
#    are not supplied by the list endpoint and existing values are preserved.
# These variables are read from saved private account connections. Signed-in users
# can import them from a file, pasted text, or Import Server .env. .env alone does not enable inventory sync
# or public Domain Search for Squarespace.
# SQUARESPACE_RESELLER_CLIENT_ID=
# SQUARESPACE_RESELLER_CLIENT_SECRET=
# These are app-specific names for the approved reseller credentials. Existing
# SQUARESPACE_CLIENT_ID / SQUARESPACE_CLIENT_SECRET values stay generic; they are
# not reclassified automatically. If those saved values were issued for your
# approved reseller account, enter them in the dedicated reseller fields.
# Domain listing does not expose an ordinary customer's existing personal
# portfolio, including migrated Google Domains, through this reseller connection.
# For personal Squarespace domains, use manual entry or CSV import. Domains
# discovered through connected Hostinger hosting are included automatically.

# UPCOMING PRO CONNECTIONS: DYNADOT, CLOUDFLARE, AND NAME.COM
# These providers are Coming Soon placeholders in Profile > Connections.
# Credential setup, .env import, domain search, and inventory sync are not enabled.
# Supported variable names and setup steps will be added here when their
# integrations are available. Do not add their credentials to this template yet.
